COPY public.evaluations (id, "interviewId", score, feedback, breakdown, "createdAt") FROM stdin;
f9c3eff9-9c97-47a3-8b6e-ffc00d9c8e24	e8e84fa4-b8a5-4f70-861a-f2562694b12e	35	The candidate demonstrates a fundamental mismatch with the Tech Lead Manager, Oracle ERP role at Airbnb across all critical dimensions. Despite an 80% resume match score, the interview reveals no meaningful Oracle ERP experience, no professional leadership or management background (relying solely on academic capstone examples), and technical depth consistent with a junior-to-mid-level engineer rather than the required Staff IC + people leadership level. The candidate shows basic competence in Node.js/PostgreSQL, REST APIs, and authentication patterns but lacks Java/Python proficiency, ERP domain knowledge (R2R, P2P, AP, AR), AI/ML experience (LLMs, RAG, Claude Code), and the strategic architectural thinking needed for multi-tenant ERP integrations at scale. Responses are consistently brief, lack the STAR structure expected for behavioral questions, and fail to address the specific context of Airbnb's Finance integration challenges. The final-year student context makes the 10+ year experience requirement impossible to meet. This candidate would require 5-7 years of targeted experience before being viable for this role.	{"relevance": 15, "strengths": ["Demonstrates foundational backend engineering skills in Node.js, PostgreSQL, and REST API design", "Shows awareness of security best practices (HTTP-only cookies, CSRF protection, idempotency keys)"], "confidence": 50, "improvements": ["Gain professional experience with Oracle ERP modules (R2R, P2P, AP, AR) and Oracle Integration Cloud (OIC)", "Develop deep proficiency in Java or Python for building production microservices and integrations", "Acquire hands-on AI/ML experience with LLMs, RAG, and agent frameworks (Claude Code, MCP)", "Build a track record of technical leadership and people management in a professional setting", "Practice structuring behavioral responses using STAR method with measurable business outcomes"], "communication": 40, "problemSolving": 40, "recommendation": "Do Not Hire - Candidate lacks the required 10+ years experience, Oracle ERP expertise, Java/Python proficiency, AI/ML background, and professional leadership experience for this Tech Lead Manager role. The gaps are fundamental and cannot be bridged without significant career development.", "technicalKnowledge": 35}	2026-09-05 16:22:32.673
fb5518d2-b14f-42aa-9101-0e1b491a3453	3b3553b9-1ec9-4065-a89f-44f86776bdfd	65	The candidate demonstrates strong backend engineering fundamentals with notable depth in database systems (Prisma/PostgreSQL optimization, migration strategies), distributed systems concepts (token bucket rate limiting, sliding window algorithms), and API design principles (GraphQL vs REST trade-offs, versioning, pagination). They excel in algorithmic problem-solving (Q11: 88) and practical system implementation (Q8: 88, Q12: 85). However, three critical failures in communication and relevance severely undermine the overall assessment: Q3, Q10, and Q13 were completely off-topic, indicating either poor listening under pressure or an inability to context-switch — a significant risk for a collaborative, cross-functional role at Stripe. Additional gaps include weak authentication security practices (Q4: no refresh tokens, revocation, or XSS mitigation), missing concurrency/versioning design (Q6), and an incomplete behavioral response (Q15). The candidate's strongest behavioral answer (Q14: 95) shows maturity in prioritization and stakeholder communication, but this is not consistent across the session. For a DEEP team role where API ergonomics, reliability, and cross-team collaboration are paramount, the communication failures and security/concurrency gaps outweigh the technical strengths.	{"relevance": 45, "strengths": ["Deep practical knowledge of PostgreSQL, Prisma ORM, and zero-downtime migration patterns", "Strong algorithmic and distributed systems thinking (sliding window unique counts, token bucket rate limiting)", "Clear understanding of GraphQL resolver architecture, N+1 problems, and DataLoader patterns", "Ability to articulate complex technical trade-offs with concrete examples (raw SQL vs ORM, REST vs GraphQL)"], "confidence": 65, "improvements": ["Critical: Improve active listening and question comprehension — three completely off-topic responses suggest difficulty processing requirements in real-time", "Strengthen API security knowledge: implement refresh token rotation, token revocation, httpOnly cookies, and XSS/CSRF defenses", "Address concurrency control and versioning strategies for collaborative editing (CRDTs, OT, optimistic locking)", "Adopt Stripe-specific API conventions: cursor-based pagination, expand patterns, idempotency keys, and standard error envelopes", "Complete behavioral responses fully and ensure they address the actual competency being assessed"], "communication": 55, "problemSolving": 80, "recommendation": "Weak Hire - Strong technical foundation in backend systems and databases, but communication failures (3/15 questions off-topic) and gaps in API security/concurrency design pose significant risk for a Stripe DEEP role where cross-functional collaboration and API ergonomics are core requirements. Consider only with extended evaluation on communication and API design.", "technicalKnowledge": 75}	2026-09-08 14:42:51.229
f91b9f4a-e456-463b-a736-8c9eb8a028d3	d8fc575f-de56-4899-a41c-2adca77caa20	73	The candidate demonstrates strong practical engineering judgment, product sense, and growth-oriented thinking — particularly in system design trade-offs, security patterns, and cross-functional collaboration. Behavioral responses (Q10, Q11) are excellent, showing clear metric alignment, ambiguity navigation, and data-driven reflection. Project deep dives reveal solid full-stack experience with thoughtful schema design, auth architecture, and pragmatic ML choices. However, critical gaps exist in core CS fundamentals: a complete misunderstanding of the JavaScript event loop (Q6, 10/100) and shallow ML lifecycle awareness (Q8, 65/100) directly contradict the role's technical expectations. While the candidate compensates with strong learning signals and pragmatic problem-solving, these gaps pose risk for a Stripe Growth engineer expected to work across the stack with rigor. The 50% resume match and identified skill gaps (algorithms, JavaScript, ML) are validated by the interview. With 2+ years experience, they meet the minimum bar but fall short of the preferred qualifications for immediate high-impact contribution.	{"relevance": 70, "strengths": ["Exceptional behavioral competency: translates business intent into measurable product utility, navigates ambiguity with user-centric trade-off frameworks, and reflects with growth mindset", "Strong practical system design: consistently proposes pragmatic, scalable architectures (hybrid async processing, multi-stage recommendation funnels, secure auth patterns) with appropriate technology choices"], "confidence": 80, "improvements": ["Fundamental JavaScript concurrency model: must master event loop, microtask/macrotask queues, and async/await scheduling — this is non-negotiable for a full-stack role at Stripe", "ML engineering lifecycle: develop depth in temporal leakage prevention, time-aware validation, feature selection, and production monitoring beyond baseline model selection"], "communication": 85, "problemSolving": 80, "recommendation": "Weak Hire — candidate shows high potential, strong product/engineering judgment, and meets minimum experience bar, but critical gaps in JavaScript fundamentals and ML rigor require significant ramp-up. Recommend hiring only with structured onboarding plan targeting these gaps and clear 90-day milestones for technical depth.", "technicalKnowledge": 65}	2026-10-25 12:44:26.428
f9616eb2-a520-4836-b5e2-323ee1a6333e	f4ad1340-d336-4e71-adf4-ea548ef87978	88	The candidate demonstrated strong proficiency in MongoDB fundamentals and applied query writing across all four questions, with scores ranging from 85 to 100. Answers were technically accurate, well-structured, and communicated clearly, reflecting a solid understanding of document modeling, primary key mechanics, query operators, and update semantics. However, the interview session was narrowly scoped to MongoDB only, leaving critical role requirements unassessed — including Ruby, MySQL, distributed systems, cloud infrastructure (AWS/Kubernetes), financial data integrity patterns, and large-scale API design. The resume match score of 13% and identified skill gaps (AWS, Go, GraphQL, Kubernetes, ML, MongoDB, MySQL) suggest the candidate may lack breadth for this full-stack financial engineering role. While MongoDB competence is a necessary component, it is not sufficient for a position requiring 8+ years of end-to-end financial systems experience.	{"relevance": 85, "strengths": ["Precise and confident MongoDB knowledge across conceptual and practical dimensions", "Clear, concise communication with correct syntax and terminology"], "confidence": 95, "improvements": ["Demonstrate experience with relational databases (MySQL/PostgreSQL), Ruby, and financial data modeling in subsequent rounds", "Showcase distributed systems design, cloud infrastructure (AWS/K8s), and large-scale API/service ownership"], "communication": 95, "problemSolving": 90, "recommendation": "Weak Hire — Strong MongoDB fundamentals but insufficient evidence of broader required competencies (Ruby, MySQL, distributed systems, cloud, financial domain). Recommend advancing only if subsequent interviews validate full-stack financial engineering experience.", "technicalKnowledge": 92}	2026-10-31 17:38:29.896
