aada9f78-69f3-4a70-b85b-4ded1297ced3	3b3553b9-1ec9-4065-a89f-44f86776bdfd	Introduction	I see you've worked with Docker, Vercel, and Render for deployments. Can you tell us about a time you had to troubleshoot a deployment issue or optimize a container build? What did you learn from that experience?	2	During a recent microservices deployment, I encountered a container crash-looping issue caused by a missing runtime environment variable required for database connection initialization. The container kept failing health checks on launch, preventing the release pipeline from completing.\n\nTo resolve this, I first analyzed the container logs via Docker/Vercel CLI logs to pinpoint the unhandled rejection. I updated our deployment configuration to validate required environment variables at runtime during server startup, throwing clear diagnostic errors if any key was missing. Additionally, I optimized our Dockerfile by using multi-stage builds and explicit layer caching for npm dependencies, which reduced the final container image size by over 40% and cut pipeline build times in half.\n\nFrom this experience, I learned the importance of strict environment validation at the entry point of microservices and the value of multi-stage container optimization for faster, more reliable CI/CD deployments.	{"score":85,"feedback":"The candidate provides a clear, structured response with a concrete example, quantified results, and relevant takeaways. However, the answer could benefit from deeper technical details on the validation implementation, Dockerfile specifics, and how the solution applies across mentioned platforms like Render.","strengths":["Clear problem identification and resolution steps","Quantified improvements (40% image size reduction, 50% build time reduction)","Relevant lessons learned tied to microservices reliability and CI/CD efficiency"],"improvements":["Provide more detail on environment validation implementation (e.g., schema validation, fail-fast mechanisms)",